labour is urging the government to  publish its exit strategy from the  coronavirus lockdown this week  Labour's leader secured stammer has told  Dominic Raab that while he supports the  lockdown extension the public need to  know what's coming next  for the sake of morale and hope and  handily enough as you can see Saki  Astana joins is no secure very good  morning to you and set out if you would  in just a little bit more detail exactly  what it is that you are calling upon the  government to provide you and colleagues  with yeah thank you  well first we know that the government  is going to say that lockdown has to  stay in place for a bit longer they're  right about that and I said when I took  over as leader Labour Party but the lay  part with that courage to say when the  government is right and support it we  will support that lockdown must continue  what I'm calling on the government to do  is to set out now what the exit strategy  is so in other words what will happen  next  I'm not asking for dates I do understand  that that's unrealistic and I said I  wouldn't ask the impossible but what we  need to know is what's the strategy what  are the criteria and I think there are  two main reasons we need to know that  the first is to make sure we've got the  trust of the public we're asking a lot  of the public it is really difficult  what they're going through whether  that's for health reasons economic  reasons or many many others we need to  maintain trust and I believe that  transparency is the best way to continue  that trust and a sense of hope about  what's going to happen next the second  reason is that whatever the strategy the  plans need to be in place sooner rather  than later  and give you just one a small example of  that I think it's likely that mass  testing and I mean mass testing and  tracing is likely to be part of any  strategy if we're to get mass testing in  to caution at the right time we need to  start the work on that now because as  you have seen the government struggled  with testing so far it promised 25,000  tests by the middle of April where we're  there now and they've only got 15,000  he's promised a hundred thousand by the  end of April  so I hope they achieve that but that's a  big curve but a hundred thousand is  nothing like the number we've need if we  were tamp this as part of the exit  strategy  so for public trust and planning now is  the time to set that strategy out and I  can say to the government if they do set  it out or we can test it and challenge  it and look at it but we think it's  broadly right we will then be able to  support it so there's a there's an added  advantage of taking this step now and  there will be people watching this who  welcome any opposition politician asking  questions which which frankly you know  journalists have been asking the daily  the daily briefing for quite some time  there will be others who point out that  you agree with the lockdown extension in  part I presume because that is the  advice coming from the chief medical  officer and the chief science officer  however when the chief medical officer  and the chief science officer tell you  it is too early to be discussing the  exit strategy from lock don't you  disagree well let me set out the basis  for disagreement because I said when I  took over a leader that we would be a  constructive opposition with the courage  to say we support the government where  we do but also with the courage to  challenge where we think there are  mistakes or things aren't happening  quickly enough and I said that the  purpose of that challenge would not be  to score points or just because we can  but to point out mistakes or things not  happening quickly enough so they can be  put right and that's why we focused  heavily in recent weeks on protective  equipment for the frontline  and on testing not to score a point but  just to say you're not where you need to  be you need to ramp it up  but I do think that mistakes were made  earlier in terms of the planning for  lockdown and the speed of lockdown let's  not repeat that I don't want to dwell on  the past  let's not repeat that let's actually  make the planning decisions for the  future science is really important in  this but science in the end can't tell  you what the political strategy is for  coming out of lockdown because that  involves political decisions as well as  scientific decisions part of part of  Labor's a request for this this extra  additional information appears to be  about providing some reassurance to  general public who in the manor are  following the rules to give them an idea  that there is some light at the end of  the tunnel what if there isn't a lot of  light at the end of the tunnel what if  we cannot see that end of the tunnel  just yet are we right to raise  expectations of a relaxation or even an  end to these lockdown conditions when  frankly as yet there is no empirical  data to suggest that the government  should be changing course well we do  need to reassure the public because I  think the final exit strategy can only  come with a vaccine because it's only at  that point that we can probably say we  can fully exit but we all know that a  vaccine or it is it was said that a  vaccine won't be available for 12 months  or so so the government has to answer  the question what happens in the 12  months or whatever that period is and I  think but the public's have trust in  what's going on they're going to need  answers to those questions I'm not  asking for timelines I do understand  that I accept the may have to be some  conditionality in this if X happens then  we would do the following whatever that  may be people understand that but what  they want to know is what is the  approach that you're going to take when  the curve flattens as we all desperately  hope that it does and the sooner the  better because people are putting up  with a lot I thank them I mean people  are doing amazing things in the national  interest we want them to continue to do  that but I think we can only do that if  we have their trust and this is  difficult for people particularly those  that are in overcrowded accommodation  those that are lonely with mental health  issues those that are in abusive  relationship are particularly at risk so  this we're asking a huge amount of the  public and I genuinely think the  transparency about what happens next and  the public will understand if there are  conditions and the public will  understand if we say you can't say  precisely what date or what time but  also those plans need to go into place  and also if we can get a consensus that  this is the right strategy going forward  I think that will get trust of the  public as well I'm secure can we turn  your attention just briefly to there to  the leaked report  to a legate into the investigation of  allegations of into anti-semitism and  within the party I mean trust things  first I spoke to the Shadow Chancellor  yesterday and she knew nothing about  this internal review of an internal  review that you yourself of announcer  why is the party reviewing a review that  it itself commissioned well there was a  leaked internal review that was leaked  yesterday or the day before when I saw  it I was concerned about it and  therefore set up an investigation into  how that came about and into the  contents of this internal review and how  it came to be leaked and that process  will now run through this is a report  into things that apparently happened  before I was leader of the Labour Party  we need an independent review it needs  to be speedy and professional but what I  said in my leadership campaign over  three months is that the Labour Party  needs to unite and focus on its historic  purpose which is Bingham government to  change millions of lives for the better  so if I pursue this I want to unite and  focus on that objective  indeed sorry for interrupting and we  have a problem with the delay on these  lines so thank you for being with me  with respect it doesn't sound as if you  have a huge amount of confidence in  again the findings of an internal  inquiry commissioned and set up by  comrades of yours within the party  carried out by comrades of yours in the  party in fact it rather suggests that  you've had a look at the findings of  this report and disagreed with them no I  haven't said that  what I've said is there needs to be an  independent investigation into the  report and the contents I will wait  until the end of that investigation  before commenting further because I  don't want to get too into this debate  about jumping to conclusions one way or  the other it needs to be looked at it  caused me serious cause for concern in  what I read so I want this investigation  I want it done speedily and  professionally but more than anything I  want our party to pull together and  focus on the real objective which is our  historic purpose the reason the Labour  Party was set up which is to go into  government and change millions of lives  for the better  are you prepared to eject people from  the party who were actively undermining  your predecessor general Cornyn I'm not  going to preempts the outcome of this  investigation I've set it up I want it  to be speedy and I'll obviously look at  the result when we get it but I'm not  gonna pre Empire sakae ozawa thanks very  much for joining us this morning hope  you and yours stay very well

